<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kagerō Nikki</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 974</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as The Gossamer Years, The Mayfly Diary</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kagerō Nikki, known in English as The Gossamer Years, is a work of classical Japanese diary literature written around 974 by a Heian noblewoman known only as the Mother of Michitsuna. Mixing waka poems and prose, it records her marriage to the courtier Fujiwara no Kaneie, her anguish as his visits to her dwindled, her pilgrimages, and her devotion to her son and adopted daughter. She probably began it around 971, during one of her marriage crises. Its frank first-person exploration of feeling expanded the possibilities of kana prose and influenced later women's writing, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-tale-of-genji">The Tale of Genji</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kagero-nikki.jpg" alt="Kagerō Nikki" width="330" height="457" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Utagawa, Kunisada, 1786-1864, artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Udaisho_michitsuna_no_haha_LCCN2008660907.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
